window.SURGERY.sections.push({
  id: "mitral-repair",
  category: "valve",
  title: "Degenerative Mitral Valve Repair",
  subtitle: "Fixing a floppy valve instead of replacing it — leaflet anatomy, the Carpentier classification, resection-and-ring technique, and the SAM problem that follows a repair that is too good.",
  readMins: 30,
  day: 8,
  blocks: [
    {
      title: "Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it",
      html: "<p>The mitral valve is the inflow valve of the left ventricle. It has to do two contradictory things well: open wide enough in diastole to admit the entire stroke volume from the left atrium in a fraction of a second, and then seal completely in systole against the highest pressure the heart generates, all while being tethered from below by a subvalvular apparatus that must relax and tense in perfect coordination with the ventricle around it. Degenerative mitral valve disease is a mechanical failure of this system — the leaflet tissue, the chordae, or both become abnormally elongated or weak, the leaflet edge prolapses above the plane of the annulus in systole, and blood regurgitates backward into the left atrium instead of forward into the aorta. This is myxomatous or degenerative mitral regurgitation (MR), and it is the most common reason a mitral valve is operated on in the developed world.</p><p>The central teaching point of this entire article is that for degenerative MR, <b>repair is preferred over replacement</b> whenever it is technically feasible, and this preference is not a stylistic one — it is backed by outcomes. A successful repair preserves the native leaflets and, critically, the subvalvular apparatus (the chordae and papillary muscles), which keeps the left ventricle's geometry, its contractile efficiency, and its long-term function intact in a way that replacement cannot fully replicate even when chordal preservation is attempted at replacement. Repair carries a lower operative mortality than replacement, avoids the lifelong risks of a prosthesis (structural deterioration for a bioprosthesis, or anticoagulation for a mechanical valve), and durable repairs performed at experienced centers now routinely last decades. This is why the single most important predictor of whether a degenerative MR patient should go to surgery early is not just the numeric severity of the leak, but whether the surgeon believes a durable repair is achievable.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> For primary (degenerative) MR, the operative decision is really two questions bundled together: is this severe enough to operate, and can this valve be repaired durably? A valve that meets severity criteria but is anatomically unfavorable for repair changes the risk-benefit calculation and the timing discussion.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby",
      html: "<p>The mitral annulus is not a flat ring — it is a three-dimensional <b>saddle shape</b>, higher anteriorly and posteriorly and lower at the two commissures, and this shape reduces leaflet and chordal stress across the cardiac cycle. The valve has two leaflets of very different shape: the <b>anterior leaflet</b> is tall, roughly semicircular, and occupies about a third of the annular circumference but around half the valve orifice area, while the <b>posterior leaflet</b> is shorter but occupies about two-thirds of the annular circumference and is naturally scalloped into three segments. By convention (the Carpentier nomenclature) the posterior leaflet segments are labelled <b>P1 (lateral/anterolateral), P2 (middle), and P3 (medial/posteromedial)</b>, and the anterior leaflet is divided into corresponding, though not truly scalloped, regions <b>A1, A2 and A3</b> directly opposite them. The leaflets meet at the <b>anterolateral and posteromedial commissures</b>.</p><p>Below the leaflets, thin fibrous cords called <b>chordae tendineae</b> anchor the leaflet edges and bodies to two <b>papillary muscles</b> arising from the left ventricular wall — the <b>anterolateral papillary muscle</b> (with a dual blood supply, usually from both the left anterior descending and circumflex territories) and the <b>posteromedial papillary muscle</b> (with a single blood supply, usually from the posterior descending artery, which is why it is more vulnerable to ischemic papillary muscle rupture after an inferior MI). Two structures outside the valve itself are the danger structures of mitral surgery: the <b>circumflex artery</b> runs in the left atrioventricular groove immediately adjacent to the posterior annulus, closest to it near <b>P1</b>, and the <b>coronary sinus</b> runs alongside it in the same groove. Annuloplasty sutures placed too deep or too far laterally along the posterior annulus, especially near P1, can kink or ligate the circumflex artery and cause an acute lateral wall myocardial infarction.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> The circumflex artery runs directly behind the posterior mitral annulus, closest to it near the P1 segment. Deep annular sutures placed laterally during annuloplasty or leaflet repair are the classic mechanism of iatrogenic circumflex injury and a new lateral wall infarct.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum",
      figures: [{"id": "mitral-repair-carpentier-motion-v2", "src": "assets/surgery-generated/mitral-repair/carpentier-motion-v2.png", "width": 1536, "height": 1024, "alt": "Three mitral valve cutaways compare annular dilation, leaflet prolapse, and leaflet restriction.", "caption": "From left to right: normal leaflet motion with an annular coaptation gap (Type I), excessive motion with prolapse (Type II), and restricted motion with chordal tethering (Type III)."}],
      html: "<p>Before you can plan any mitral repair you must classify how the leaflets are moving, because the mechanism dictates the fix. The <b>Carpentier classification</b> describes leaflet motion, not the underlying cause: <b>Type I</b> is normal leaflet motion with a leak from annular dilation or a leaflet perforation (as in endocarditis); <b>Type II</b> is excessive leaflet motion — prolapse, where part of the leaflet edge rises above the annular plane in systole, the hallmark of degenerative disease; <b>Type IIIa</b> is restricted leaflet motion in both diastole and systole, typically from rheumatic thickening and chordal fusion; and <b>Type IIIb</b> is restricted motion specifically in systole, the pattern of ischemic or functional MR where a dilated, tethering ventricle pulls the leaflets down and apart. Degenerative MR is therefore a <b>Type II</b> lesion, and everything in this article about resection, neochordae and annuloplasty is aimed at correcting excess leaflet motion while preserving or restoring normal coaptation.</p><p>Within degenerative disease itself there is a spectrum worth naming. <b>Fibroelastic deficiency (FED)</b> is a disease of deficient connective tissue: the leaflet tissue is thin, often near-normal in most of the valve, with a single ruptured or elongated chord producing a focal prolapse of one segment (very commonly P2), usually in an older patient with a valve that was otherwise structurally unremarkable until that one chord failed. <b>Barlow disease</b> is the opposite extreme: a diffuse excess of myxomatous tissue producing thick, redundant, billowing leaflets across multiple segments, often with annular dilation and elongation of many chordae rather than one discrete rupture, usually presenting in younger patients and technically more demanding to repair because more segments and more chordae are abnormal. Most degenerative valves fall somewhere on the spectrum between these two poles, and where a valve sits changes both the technical plan and the difficulty of achieving a durable repair.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> Carpentier in one line — <b>I normal, II too much, IIIa stuck shut (diastole, rheumatic), IIIb stuck open (systole, ischemic/functional)</b>. Degenerative MR is always Type II.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR",
      html: "<p>Chronic severe MR is deceptively well tolerated for years because the left atrium and, to a degree, the left ventricle can accommodate the regurgitant volume by dilating, so many patients remain asymptomatic despite a severe leak on echo. This is precisely why timing surgery correctly matters so much: operate too late, after the ventricle has begun to remodel and fail from chronic volume overload, and even a technically perfect repair will not fully restore normal ventricular function; operate on a valve that will not repair durably, and you may have converted the patient into a replacement candidate they did not need to become yet. Diagnosis and severity grading rest on <b>transthoracic echocardiography</b> (with transesophageal imaging used intraoperatively and whenever transthoracic images are inadequate), assessing regurgitant volume and fraction, effective regurgitant orifice area, vena contracta width, pulmonary vein flow reversal, and left atrial and ventricular size, together with the mechanism (which leaflet segment prolapses, and why).</p><p>The 2020 ACC/AHA valve guideline indications for intervention in <b>severe primary (degenerative) MR</b> are built around this logic. Surgery is indicated for <b>symptomatic</b> severe primary MR regardless of LV function (assuming the patient is an operative candidate). In the <b>asymptomatic</b> patient, surgery is indicated when there is evidence the ventricle is beginning to be affected — an <b>LVEF between 30 and 60 percent</b>, or a <b>left ventricular end-systolic dimension (LVESD) of 40 mm or more</b> — because these are the measurable signs that irreversible remodeling may be starting even without symptoms. New-onset <b>atrial fibrillation</b> or new <b>pulmonary hypertension</b> (resting pulmonary artery systolic pressure over 50 mmHg) in an asymptomatic patient with normal LV size and function are also triggers to operate, because they signal that the chronic volume load is already producing downstream consequences. Finally, and distinctively for this lesion, the guideline explicitly endorses <b>earlier surgery in an asymptomatic patient with normal LV size and function when the likelihood of a durable repair is high and the procedure can be performed at a Heart Valve Center of excellence with expected low mortality</b> — an acknowledgment that when repair (not replacement) with very low risk is achievable, waiting for the ventricle to declare damage is not necessary.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> The 2020 guideline's endorsement of early surgery for a high-probability-of-repair asymptomatic patient at an experienced center is unique to degenerative MR among the valve lesions — it exists specifically because a durable repair removes most of the downside risk that would otherwise argue for watchful waiting.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation",
      figures: [{"id": "mitral-repair-resection-vs-neochord-v1", "src": "assets/surgery-generated/mitral-repair/resection-vs-neochord-v1.png", "width": 1536, "height": 1024, "alt": "Two mitral repair approaches: posterior leaflet resection with sutured edges and artificial chordae from papillary muscle to posterior leaflet.", "caption": "Resection removes a prolapsing posterior leaflet segment and rejoins the edges; neochordae restore support without removing leaflet tissue. Both are paired with annular stabilization."}],
      html: "<p>Every degenerative mitral repair combines two separate ideas: correcting the prolapsing or excess leaflet tissue itself, and stabilizing the annulus so the repair does not fail as the ventricle and annulus continue to move. For the leaflet, the classic technique for a focal posterior leaflet prolapse (commonly P2) is <b>triangular or quadrangular resection</b> — the redundant, prolapsing segment is excised as a wedge or rectangle and the remaining edges are sewn back together, restoring a normal height and eliminating the excess tissue that was billowing above the coaptation line. When a wider segment is resected, a <b>sliding leaflet plasty</b> detaches the leaflet from the annulus on either side of the resection, slides the remaining leaflet tissue together, and reattaches it to the annulus, reducing overall leaflet height so the repaired segment does not stand taller than its neighbors and produce a new, secondary prolapse.</p><p>An increasingly dominant alternative, especially useful for anterior leaflet prolapse (where resection risks creating outflow tract obstruction or an unstable leaflet) or when native chordae are simply too few or too degenerated to preserve, is <b>artificial neochordae</b> — loops of expanded PTFE (Gore-Tex) suture placed from the papillary muscle head to the free edge of the prolapsing segment, replacing the function of ruptured or elongated native chordae without removing any leaflet tissue at all. A third technique, the <b>edge-to-edge repair (Alfieri stitch)</b>, sutures the free edges of the anterior and posterior leaflets together at the point of worst prolapse or flail, creating a double-orifice valve; it is simple, quick, and useful as an adjunct or a bailout, though used alone it can under-treat wide or multi-segment pathology and today usually complements rather than replaces resection or neochordae. Whichever leaflet technique is chosen, essentially <b>every degenerative repair is completed with a ring or band annuloplasty</b>, an implant sewn around the annulus that reduces and stabilizes its dimension, restores the correct anterior-to-posterior geometry, and protects the leaflet repair from later annular dilation; the ring is sized to the anterior leaflet (specifically its inter-trigonal distance and surface area), because the anterior leaflet's dimensions are the stable reference point in a valve where the posterior annulus is what dilates.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> The modern degenerative-repair toolkit — <b>resect (triangular/quadrangular +/- sliding plasty), replace (PTFE neochordae), reinforce the edge (Alfieri), and always ring</b> (annuloplasty ring/band sized to the anterior leaflet). Most repairs use two or three of these in combination.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The operation, step by step",
      html: "<p>Mitral repair is performed on cardiopulmonary bypass with a still, decompressed, open heart, whether approached through a median sternotomy or a minimally invasive right thoracotomy/port approach; the exposure differs but the intracardiac steps are the same. Bicaval venous cannulation with caval snares is used (not a single two-stage cannula), because the left atrium must be opened and kept dry of all venous return during the repair, and the aorta is cross-clamped with cardioplegic arrest as in any open-chamber procedure.</p><p>The left atrium is opened, most commonly through the interatrial groove (Sondergaard's groove) posterior to the atrial septum, or via a trans-septal approach when exposure of the mitral valve needs to be improved (for example in a small left atrium or in a reoperation). A retractor is placed to expose the valve, and the surgeon systematically inspects every leaflet segment, testing coaptation and chordal integrity with a nerve hook or saline injected retrograde into the ventricle to reproduce systolic pressure and confirm exactly which segment prolapses or is flail — this careful segmental analysis is what determines the specific repair plan (resection with or without sliding plasty, neochordae, edge-to-edge, or a combination). The chosen leaflet repair is performed first, then sizers are used to select an annuloplasty ring or band matched to the anterior leaflet dimension, and the ring is sewn circumferentially around the annulus with interrupted or running sutures, again with particular care along the posterior annulus near P1 to avoid the circumflex artery.</p><p>Once the ring is seated, competence is tested directly in the arrested heart by re-injecting saline under pressure to confirm a symmetric coaptation line and no residual jet, the left atriotomy is closed, and the heart is de-aired and weaned from bypass exactly as in any open-chamber case. The single most important quality-control step of the entire operation happens after weaning: <b>intraoperative transesophageal echocardiography</b> is used to confirm the repair is competent (no more than trivial residual MR), that there is no systolic anterior motion of the anterior leaflet causing dynamic outflow obstruction, and that the mitral valve area is not iatrogenically stenotic — if any of these is unsatisfactory, the surgeon returns to bypass and revises the repair (or, rarely, converts to replacement) before leaving the operating room, because a mitral repair is not considered complete until it has passed this on-table echo test.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> A degenerative mitral repair is never \"done\" until post-repair TEE confirms it. This on-table verification, with a low threshold to return to bypass and revise, is what separates a durable repair from one that fails within the first postoperative year.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management",
      figures: [{"id": "mitral-repair-sam-outflow-v1", "src": "assets/surgery-generated/mitral-repair/sam-outflow-v1.png", "width": 1536, "height": 1024, "alt": "Paired left-ventricular cutaways compare anterior mitral leaflet movement toward the outflow tract with a leaflet that stays clear of it.", "caption": "After repair, anterior displacement of the mitral leaflet can narrow the left-ventricular outflow tract and disrupt coaptation. The paired view contrasts this with an unobstructed tract."}],
      html: "<p>Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve is a dynamic complication in which the anterior leaflet (or the repaired posterior leaflet edge, pulled by chordae) is drawn anteriorly into the left ventricular outflow tract during systole, obstructing outflow and typically dragging the mitral valve open enough to produce a new, often severe, posteriorly directed regurgitant jet. It happens because a repair — most often one using a large ring that reduces the annulus aggressively, combined with a posterior leaflet that is left too tall — leaves surplus leaflet coaptation tissue positioned close to the outflow tract, and this redundant leaflet is then caught by the high-velocity systolic flow stream (the Venturi effect draws it into the outflow path) in a ventricle that is small, hypercontractile, or under low afterload, all of which narrow the outflow tract or accelerate flow through it.</p><p>The classic risk profile for post-repair SAM is a small hyperdynamic left ventricle, a redundant posterior leaflet with excessive height, systolic anterior displacement of the coaptation point, and a hypovolemic or vasodilated patient at the moment of weaning from bypass — exactly the physiologic state a freshly bypassed patient can be in. The first-line management is therefore entirely medical and physiologic, addressed in a specific order: give <b>volume</b> to fill and enlarge the ventricle, <b>increase afterload</b> with a vasoconstrictor (raising resistance widens the outflow tract and slows the Venturi-accelerated flow that drags the leaflet into it), and <b>reduce or stop inotropes</b>, since a hypercontractile ventricle worsens the obstruction. Beta blockade can also be used to reduce contractility and heart rate. If SAM and its resultant MR persist despite these maneuvers, the repair itself must be revised on bypass — options include converting to a larger annuloplasty ring (paradoxically, an undersized ring is a common contributor), a sliding plasty to reduce posterior leaflet height, or, if all else fails, replacement.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> New systolic anterior motion with dynamic outflow obstruction and a new MR jet after weaning from bypass following mitral repair should never be treated with inotropes or diuresis — both make it worse. The correct sequence is volume, increase afterload, reduce inotropy, and if it persists, return to bypass and revise the repair.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Complications and their management",
      html: "<p>Beyond SAM, the complications of degenerative mitral repair track the anatomy and technique described above. <b>Circumflex artery injury</b> from an annuloplasty suture placed too deep or too laterally near P1 presents as new regional wall-motion abnormality, ST-segment change, or hemodynamic instability at the time of weaning, and is recognized on intraoperative echo or ECG change; management ranges from suture removal and revision if caught immediately, to coronary bypass grafting if the injury is not recognized until later and the myocardium is jeopardized. <b>Residual MR</b> after repair — from an incompletely corrected prolapse, a missed second segment of pathology, or a ring that is too small or too large — is why post-repair TEE is mandatory; more than mild residual MR on the table is generally an indication to revise before leaving the OR, since residual MR at the time of surgery predicts earlier failure of the repair.</p><p><b>Atrioventricular groove disruption</b> is a rare but catastrophic complication, a tear through the tissue plane between the atrium and ventricle at the posterior annulus, most often provoked by excessive traction, aggressive debridement of calcium, or an oversized prosthesis/ring during exposure or repair; it presents as sudden, torrential hemorrhage from behind the heart and requires immediate recognition, return to bypass if not already on it, and meticulous repair often reinforced with pericardial patches — prevention through gentle technique and appropriate sizing is far more reliable than any rescue. <b>Iatrogenic mitral stenosis from over-resection</b> occurs when too much leaflet tissue is removed relative to the annuloplasty ring size, or when the ring itself is undersized, leaving too little leaflet area or annular circumference to admit normal diastolic flow; it is avoided by matching resection extent and ring size to the anterior leaflet dimension and confirming an adequate diastolic mitral valve area (and low mean gradient) on post-repair echo, not merely confirming the absence of a regurgitant jet.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> A technically successful repair is judged by two numbers on post-repair echo, not one: no more than trivial regurgitation AND a non-stenotic diastolic valve area. Chasing a perfect systolic seal by over-resecting or over-reducing the annulus can silently trade regurgitation for stenosis.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument",
      html: "<p>Own the <b>Carpentier classification</b> cold: Type I (normal motion, annular dilation or perforation), Type II (excess motion/prolapse — degenerative MR), Type IIIa (restricted in diastole — rheumatic), Type IIIb (restricted in systole — ischemic/functional). Know the degenerative spectrum by name: <b>fibroelastic deficiency</b> (focal, usually single chordal rupture, often P2, near-normal surrounding tissue) versus <b>Barlow disease</b> (diffuse myxomatous excess, multiple segments, annular dilation, technically harder to repair). Know the mitral segment nomenclature (<b>A1/A2/A3</b> anterior opposite <b>P1/P2/P3</b> posterior) and that the annuloplasty ring is sized to the <b>anterior leaflet</b>, because it is the stable reference dimension in a valve where the posterior annulus is what dilates.</p><p>Know the 2020 ACC/AHA thresholds for surgery in severe primary MR verbatim: symptomatic (any LV function, Class I); asymptomatic with <b>LVEF 30 to 60 percent</b> or <b>LVESD 40 mm or more</b> (Class I); new atrial fibrillation or pulmonary hypertension with normal LV size/function (Class IIa); and early surgery in the fully asymptomatic, normal-ventricle patient when repair is highly likely and can be performed at a Heart Valve Center with expected low mortality (Class IIa) — a threshold that exists specifically because repair (not replacement) changes the risk calculus for early intervention. Know why <b>repair beats replacement</b> for degenerative MR: preserved subvalvular apparatus maintains LV geometry and long-term function, lower operative mortality, no prosthesis-related lifelong risk, and durable results at experienced centers. And know the <b>SAM</b> algorithm cold, because it is one of the highest-yield pimping sequences in cardiac surgery: mechanism is a redundant leaflet drawn into a Venturi-accelerated outflow stream in a small, hypercontractile, underfilled ventricle; management is volume, then increased afterload, then reduced inotropy, then surgical revision (larger ring, reduce posterior leaflet height via sliding plasty) if it persists.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> SAM fix, in order — <b>fill it, squeeze it (afterload up), calm it (inotropes down)</b>, and only then, if it persists, <b>fix it</b> on bypass.</div>"
    }
  ],
  steps: [
    "Confirm severe primary (degenerative) MR by TTE/TEE, define the mechanism (Carpentier type and prolapsing segment) and assess repairability",
    "Confirm indication — symptomatic MR, or asymptomatic with LVEF 30-60%, LVESD >=40 mm, new AF/pulmonary hypertension, or a high-probability repair candidate at an experienced center",
    "Position supine, perform median sternotomy (or minimally invasive right thoracotomy/port access) and open the pericardium",
    "Heparinize, confirm ACT, cannulate the aorta and perform bicaval venous cannulation with caval snares",
    "Initiate cardiopulmonary bypass, cross-clamp the aorta and arrest the heart with cardioplegia",
    "Open the left atrium (interatrial groove or trans-septal approach) and place a retractor to expose the mitral valve",
    "Systematically inspect every leaflet segment and test with saline injection to confirm the exact prolapsing or flail segment",
    "Perform the leaflet repair appropriate to the pathology: triangular/quadrangular resection with or without sliding plasty, artificial PTFE neochordae, and/or an edge-to-edge stitch",
    "Size an annuloplasty ring or band to the anterior leaflet dimension",
    "Sew the annuloplasty ring circumferentially around the annulus, taking care along the posterior annulus near P1 to avoid the circumflex artery",
    "Re-test competence with pressurized saline injection to confirm symmetric coaptation and no residual jet",
    "Close the left atriotomy",
    "De-air the heart under TEE guidance and release the aortic cross-clamp to reperfuse the myocardium",
    "Rewarm, restore a perfusing rhythm, and perform a trial off bypass",
    "Obtain post-repair TEE to confirm no more than trivial residual MR, no systolic anterior motion, and a non-stenotic mitral valve area",
    "If the repair is unsatisfactory, return to bypass and revise (or, rarely, convert to replacement) before leaving the operating room",
    "Give protamine, achieve hemostasis, decannulate, place chest drains and pacing wires, and close the chest"
  ]
});

